# Workshop Signup App

I'll build this as a single Node.js app with SQLite (which gives us real transactions and atomic guarantees without external dependencies). Here's the complete implementation:

## Project Structure

```
workshop-signup/
├── package.json
├── server.js
└── public/
    └── index.html
```

## `package.json`

```json
{
  "name": "workshop-signup",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node server.js"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "better-sqlite3": "^11.3.0",
    "express": "^4.19.2"
  }
}
```

## `server.js`

```javascript
import express from 'express';
import Database from 'better-sqlite3';
import path from 'node:path';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';

const __dirname = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
const app = express();
const db = new Database(path.join(__dirname, 'workshop.db'));

// ---------- Schema ----------
db.pragma('journal_mode = WAL');
db.pragma('foreign_keys = ON');
db.exec(`
  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS seats (
    seat_no INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
    name    TEXT NOT NULL,
    email   TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    created_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
  );
`);

const SEAT_LIMIT = 20;

// ---------- Atomic signup transaction ----------
// Wrapped in IMMEDIATE transaction so the seat-count check and the insert
// are a single atomic unit. The UNIQUE constraint on email handles dupes.
const signupTx = db.transaction((name, email) => {
  const { count } = db.prepare('SELECT COUNT(*) AS count FROM seats').get();
  if (count >= SEAT_LIMIT) {
    const err = new Error('No seats left');
    err.code = 'FULL';
    throw err;
  }
  // Seat number = next available slot; PK enforces uniqueness.
  const nextSeat = count + 1;
  try {
    db.prepare(
      'INSERT INTO seats (seat_no, name, email) VALUES (?, ?, ?)'
    ).run(nextSeat, name, email);
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.code === 'SQLITE_CONSTRAINT_UNIQUE') {
      const err = new Error('Email already registered');
      err.code = 'DUP_EMAIL';
      throw err;
    }
    throw e;
  }
  return nextSeat;
});

// Force IMMEDIATE acquisition of the write lock up-front so concurrent
// writers serialize correctly even across processes.
const signup = (name, email) => signupTx.immediate(name, email);

// ---------- Middleware ----------
app.use(express.json({ limit: '4kb' }));
app.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, 'public')));

// ---------- Routes ----------
app.get('/api/seats', (req, res) => {
  const { count } = db.prepare('SELECT COUNT(*) AS count FROM seats').get();
  // Only expose counts — no participant PII.
  res.json({ taken: count, total: SEAT_LIMIT, available: SEAT_LIMIT - count });
});

app.post('/api/signup', (req, res) => {
  const { name, email } = req.body ?? {};

  // Basic validation
  if (typeof name !== 'string' || typeof email !== 'string') {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Name and email are required.' });
  }
  const cleanName = name.trim().slice(0, 120);
  const cleanEmail = email.trim().toLowerCase().slice(0, 254);
  if (cleanName.length === 0) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Please enter your name.' });
  }
  // Deliberately simple email shape check
  if (!/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(cleanEmail)) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Please enter a valid email address.' });
  }

  try {
    const seat = signup(cleanName, cleanEmail);
    const remaining = SEAT_LIMIT -
      db.prepare('SELECT COUNT(*) AS c FROM seats').get().c;
    return res.status(201).json({ seat, remaining });
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.code === 'FULL') {
      return res.status(409).json({ error: 'Sorry — all 20 seats are taken.' });
    }
    if (e.code === 'DUP_EMAIL') {
      return res.status(409).json({
        error: 'That email is already signed up for a seat.',
      });
    }
    console.error(e);
    return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Something went wrong.' });
  }
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Listening on http://localhost:${PORT}`));
```

## `public/index.html`

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>Workshop Signup</title>
  <style>
    :root { color-scheme: light dark; }
    body {
      font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI, Roboto, sans-serif;
      max-width: 460px; margin: 3rem auto; padding: 0 1rem;
    }
    h1 { margin-bottom: .25rem; }
    .seats { margin: .5rem 0 1.5rem; font-size: 1.1rem; }
    form { display: grid; gap: .75rem; }
    label { font-weight: 600; font-size: .9rem; }
    input {
      padding: .65rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 1px solid #888;
      border-radius: 8px; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;
    }
    button {
      padding: .75rem; font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600;
      border: 0; border-radius: 8px; cursor: pointer;
      background: #2563eb; color: white;
    }
    button:disabled { background: #94a3b8; cursor: not-allowed; }
    .msg { margin-top: 1rem; padding: .75rem; border-radius: 8px; }
    .ok  { background: #dcfce7; color: #166534; }
    .err { background: #fee2e2; color: #991b1b; }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      .ok  { background: #14532d; color: #dcfce7; }
      .err { background: #7f1d1d; color: #fee2e2; }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Workshop Signup</h1>
  <p class="seats" id="seats">Loading seats…</p>

  <form id="form">
    <div>
      <label for="name">Your name</label>
      <input id="name" name="name" autocomplete="name" required maxlength="120" />
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="email">Email address</label>
      <input id="email" name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required maxlength="254" />
    </div>
    <button id="submit" type="submit">Reserve my seat</button>
  </form>
  <div id="msg" class="msg" hidden></div>

  <script>
    const seatsEl = document.getElementById('seats');
    const form    = document.getElementById('form');
    const submit  = document.getElementById('submit');
    const msgEl   = document.getElementById('msg');

    async function refreshSeats() {
      try {
        const r = await fetch('/api/seats');
        const { available, total } = await r.json();
        seatsEl.textContent =
          available > 0
            ? `${available} of ${total} seats available`
            : `All ${total} seats are taken`;
        submit.disabled = available === 0;
        if (available === 0) submit.textContent = 'Workshop is full';
      } catch {
        seatsEl.textContent = 'Could not load seat count';
      }
    }

    function showMsg(text, ok) {
      msgEl.textContent = text;
      msgEl.className = 'msg ' + (ok ? 'ok' : 'err');
      msgEl.hidden = false;
    }

    form.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      submit.disabled = true;
      const name = form.name.value;
      const email = form.email.value;
      try {
        const r = await fetch('/api/signup', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
          body: JSON.stringify({ name, email }),
        });
        const data = await r.json();
        if (r.ok) {
          showMsg(
            `You're in! Your seat number is ${data.seat}. ` +
            `(${data.remaining} seat(s) remaining)`,
            true
          );
          form.reset();
        } else {
          showMsg(data.error || 'Signup failed.', false);
        }
      } catch {
        showMsg('Network error — please try again.', false);
      } finally {
        await refreshSeats();
      }
    });

    refreshSeats();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

## How the requirements are met

**Atomic seat check + booking.** All signups go through `signupTx.immediate()`. In SQLite, `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` acquires the write lock immediately, so two concurrent requests can't both observe `count = 19` and both insert. The `SELECT COUNT(*)` and `INSERT` execute inside one serialized transaction. Because `better-sqlite3` is synchronous, even within one Node process there's no interleaving.

**One seat per email.** The `UNIQUE` constraint on `seats.email` is the source of truth. Duplicates throw `SQLITE_CONSTRAINT_UNIQUE`, which surfaces as a 409. Emails are also lowercased on input so `Foo@x.com` and `foo@x.com` collide. (For even stronger canonicalization you could normalize Gmail dots/plus-tags, but that's usually overreach.)

**PII privacy.** `/api/seats` returns only `{ taken, total, available }` — no names or emails. There's no endpoint that lists participants at all. The only thing a signer-upper learns about their own earlier entries is their own seat number from their own successful response.

**Seat numbers are stable.** Each seat is numbered 1–20 in signup order, and the primary key guarantees no two people get the same number even if a transaction were retried.

## Running it

```bash
npm install
npm start
# open http://localhost:3000
```

## Notes & limits

- **Single-node deployment.** This is correct for one server process. For horizontal scaling you'd swap SQLite for Postgres and use a `SERIALIZABLE` transaction (or `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` on a counter row), plus a unique index on `email`. The transaction boundary in `server.js` is the only thing that needs to change.
- **Rate limiting** isn't included — if this is public, put it behind a reverse proxy with per-IP throttling or add `express-rate-limit`, and consider requiring email confirmation before the seat is finalized if spam is a concern.
- **Validation** is intentionally light (shape check on email, length caps). Add captcha/turnstile if it'll be internet-facing.

